*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
# Extension package digests bind raw contribution bytes. Keep text resources stable on Windows.
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-jpd/*.json text eol=lf
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-jpd/**/*.json text eol=lf
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-jpd/**/*.md text eol=lf
# Same reason, same mechanism: this package's digests bind raw contribution bytes, and without
# this rule a Windows checkout rewrites them and GHEX005_DIGEST fails for every contribution.
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/*.json text eol=lf
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/**/*.json text eol=lf
extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/**/*.md text eol=lf
# The benchmark corpus binds RAW bytes the same way (frozen_files_digest over oracle/
# objective/retrieval files), and the rule travels WITH the corpus: #594 moved it out of the
# package to tools/development-benchmark/corpus/ and the eol rule stayed behind on the old
# path, so the next Windows checkout rewrote every corpus file to CRLF and the oracle freeze
# broke on main. A digest over raw bytes needs its eol rule pinned to WHERE THE BYTES LIVE.
tools/development-benchmark/corpus/**/*.json text eol=lf
core/architect/fixtures/** text eol=lf
tools/development-benchmark/corpus/**/*.md text eol=lf
# schemas/*.json is DELIBERATELY ABSENT from this file, and the absence is load-bearing.
#
# Those documents are pinned by a CANONICAL digest, not a raw one: schema_digest() in
# core/schema-evolution/src/canonical.rs parses the JSON, canonicalises it, and hashes THAT. So the
# line endings cannot affect the pin. Measured on schemas/event-envelope.schema.json:
#
#     raw sha256    LF fe7ee629...   CRLF ef8d3521...   <- raw hashing sees the difference
#     canonical     LF b45ddada...   CRLF b45ddada...   <- the catalog pin does not
#
# The extension packages above are the opposite case: GHEX005 binds the RAW contribution bytes, so
# without their eol=lf rules a Windows checkout rewrites them and every contribution digest fails.
#
# Do not add schemas/*.json here by analogy with them. It would be harmless, and it would be a
# wrong model made durable - and a wrong model that does not hurt is the kind that survives.
# M06 demonstrations: recorded artifacts commit byte-exact (checksummed).
docs/acceptance/demos/** -text

# `.gitattributes` covers ITSELF, and that line is here because its absence already cost a review.
# The first version of this commit was written on a checkout where this file had been smudged to
# CRLF; the editor preserved those bytes and appended LF lines, producing MIXED endings -- in the
# one file whose entire subject is line-ending consistency. `git show --check` exited 2 on it. A
# rule that governs every script but not the file stating the rule leaves the next editor to
# repeat this exactly.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Executable scripts are LF because CRLF is a FUNCTIONAL defect in them, not a cosmetic one: a
# carriage return in a shebang stops it resolving, and a stray CR surviving into a variable
# compares unequal to the value it looks identical to. This is not hypothetical here.
# The retired `slot-claim.sh` runner script carried hand-written CR and BOM stripping because the
# problem was met and worked around IN THE SCRIPT instead of being fixed at the path -- and a workaround in one
# script does nothing for the next one written.
#
# Measured before this rule: 23 of the repository's 29 committed scripts had NO eol attribute and
# stood at `i/lf w/crlf` -- LF in the index, CRLF in the working copy, varying by machine.
#
# The six `docs/acceptance/**/recipe.sh` are NOT covered: their own directory-local .gitattributes
# sets `-text`, because those runs are recorded artifacts that commit byte-exact. A deeper
# .gitattributes wins over this one, so that exemption survives these lines -- verified with
# `git check-attr`, not assumed.
#
# EXISTING checkouts do not heal themselves. Git rewrites a working-tree file when its BLOB
# changes; an attributes-only commit changes no blob, so a worktree that already holds CRLF
# scripts keeps them after pulling this, and `git status` reads clean. Every NEW checkout is
# correct from here on; only the ones that already exist need a one-time refresh.
#
# The safe refresh is a FRESH CLONE. That is deliberately the only recipe written here.
#
# A copy-paste in-place migration was drafted and withdrawn: five review rounds found five distinct
# ways for it to fail, ending in one that DELETES an operator's scripts and restores none of them
# when any path is marked skip-worktree. The failures are recorded in #676, which owns shipping a
# tested migration script. A procedure with that failure surface is a program, not a comment: it
# needs an index-state filter and its own tests, and a comment block can carry neither.
#
# The short version, so nobody re-derives the withdrawn recipe: `git add --renormalize .` changes
# nothing here (the blobs are already LF in the index), `git checkout --force` is documented only
# as "throw away local modifications" and will silently destroy uncommitted edits inside scripts,
# and any delete-then-restore form must exclude index-hidden paths before it deletes anything.
#
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# The process documents every session reads are pinned to LF so a fresh clone materialises them
# the same way on every platform instead of asking the cloner's `core.autocrlf` (#1010). An
# attributes-only change does not heal an existing CRLF working copy; it only fixes fresh checkouts.
AGENTS.md text eol=lf
docs/process/*.md text eol=lf

# The demo exemption is RESTATED here, after the rules above, because in .gitattributes the LAST
# matching line wins per attribute. `docs/acceptance/demos/** -text` sits earlier in this file, so
# without this restatement a `.sh`, `.ps1` or `.py` recorded inside a demo would resolve to
# `text: set` and be normalised -- and those directories are byte-exact by design, hashed raw by
# `tools/acceptance-map/src/bin/record_demonstration.rs`. Verified with `git check-attr`, which
# reported `text: set` for such a path before this line existed.
docs/acceptance/demos/** -text
# #1065: the frozen context-quality corpus. The fixture manifest pins each file by sha256 and
# the test re-derives the digests from the bytes on disk, so a checkout that rewrote line
# endings would read as a moved corpus on Windows alone.
adapters/tool-host/tests/fixtures/context-quality/** text eol=lf
# #1138: a UTF-16 gate transcript fixture; its BOM and NULs are the point of the file.
apps/cli/tests/fixtures/classify-red/flake-utf16.log.txt binary
